1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:02,284
A ring. A coin.

2
00:00:02,324 --> 00:00:02,819
A necklace.

3
00:00:02,859 --> 00:00:04,267
Three golden
things.

4
00:00:04,307 --> 00:00:06,227
Now watch:
melt them down.

5
00:00:06,267 --> 00:00:08,160
How many golds
do you have now?

6
00:00:08,603 --> 00:00:10,045
Three? One?

7
00:00:10,085 --> 00:00:11,494
The question
doesn’t even work.

8
00:00:11,534 --> 00:00:12,955
And that’s
the whole lesson,

9
00:00:12,995 --> 00:00:14,593
with no
memorising.

10
00:00:15,237 --> 00:00:17,205
Case two:
materials.

11
00:00:17,245 --> 00:00:18,850
Why do we say
‘made of gold’,

12
00:00:18,890 --> 00:00:20,627
and never ‘made
of golds’?

13
00:00:21,270 --> 00:00:23,025
Proof one:
the cut test.

14
00:00:23,065 --> 00:00:24,385
Cut a ring
in half.

15
00:00:24,425 --> 00:00:25,733
Is it still
a ring?

16
00:00:25,773 --> 00:00:27,764
No.
It’s a broken ring.

17
00:00:28,303 --> 00:00:30,262
Now cut a lump
of gold in half.

18
00:00:30,302 --> 00:00:31,982
Still gold? Yes.

19
00:00:32,022 --> 00:00:33,023
Cut it again.

20
00:00:33,063 --> 00:00:33,953
Still gold.

21
00:00:33,993 --> 00:00:36,261
However small,
it’s still gold.

22
00:00:37,403 --> 00:00:38,139
Proof two.

23
00:00:38,179 --> 00:00:39,671
So how do we
give a number?

24
00:00:39,711 --> 00:00:40,919
Count the objects:

25
00:00:40,959 --> 00:00:42,162
three rings.

26
00:00:42,202 --> 00:00:43,678
Or measure
the material:

27
00:00:43,718 --> 00:00:45,323
twenty grams
of gold.

28
00:00:45,363 --> 00:00:47,521
The number never
lands on gold.

29
00:00:48,670 --> 00:00:50,982
Proof three,
the twist.

30
00:00:51,022 --> 00:00:51,891
Bricks can
be counted:

31
00:00:51,931 --> 00:00:53,796
this wall has
forty bricks.

32
00:00:53,836 --> 00:00:55,444
But brick is also
a material,

33
00:00:55,484 --> 00:00:56,844
like gold.

34
00:00:57,270 --> 00:00:58,793
So both are right.

35
00:00:58,833 --> 00:01:00,940
‘Made of bricks’
means the blocks.

36
00:01:00,980 --> 00:01:03,210
‘Made of brick’
means the material.

37
00:01:03,250 --> 00:01:05,348
The meaning decides
the grammar.

38
00:01:06,470 --> 00:01:07,567
Your turn: wood.

39
00:01:07,607 --> 00:01:08,705
Cut a plank
in half.

40
00:01:08,745 --> 00:01:10,286
Still wood? Yes.

41
00:01:10,326 --> 00:01:10,642
So:

42
00:01:10,682 --> 00:01:12,628
‘This table
is made of wood.’

43
00:01:13,570 --> 00:01:14,910
So don’t memorise.

44
00:01:14,950 --> 00:01:16,269
Cut it in half.

45
00:01:16,309 --> 00:01:17,572
If every
piece is still

46
00:01:17,612 --> 00:01:19,928
the same stuff,
it’s a material.

47
00:01:19,968 --> 00:01:21,840
Measure it,
don’t count it.

